        "original_id": "6c188ec5-4571-51fc-8eef-143aa2dcaeb0",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Cases: Slaughter-House Cases; United States v. Darby Lumber Co.\n- Concepts: early civil rights law; Fourteenth Amendment; privileges or immunities; Commerce Clause\n- Laws: Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938\n\nFacts:\n- The Slaughter-House Cases were a pivotal case in early civil rights law.\n- The Slaughter-House Cases held that the Fourteenth Amendment protects the privileges or immunities of citizenship of the United States, not the privileges and immunities of citizenship of a state.\n- In 1941, United States v. Darby Lumber Co., 312 U.S. 100, upheld the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938.\n- In 1941, United States v. Darby Lumber Co. held that the U.S. Congress had power under the Commerce Clause to regulate employment conditions.",
